Make the native Mesa context backend's exported functions weak (GLX --
the one file Linux and macOS share since patch 02 was dropped -- plus
macOS's dllname) so the embed library can supply its own backend
(embed/mglcntx_embed.c) in the same link while qemu-system keeps the
native one. Never both strong: qemu-system links only
the native file, libqemu-embed links both and the strong embed definitions win.

Windows cannot do it that way (2026-09-06, M11): a COFF weak external is
not an ELF weak definition, and a weakly-defined MGLCreateContext leaves
qemu-system-i386.exe with an undefined reference rather than a fallback.
So hw/mesa/mglcntx_mingw.c is split instead — its WGL backend behind
MESAGL_WGL_BACKEND, its platform-independent helpers behind the negation
— and patch 10's meson hunk compiles the file a second time, backend
half only, into the emulators alone. The static library both consumers
share ends up holding the helpers and no backend at all, which is what
the weak marks were trying to arrange.

